DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The ssttrrppttiimmee() function is the converse of ssttrrffttiimmee(3); it converts the
       character string pointed to by _s to values  which  are  stored  in  the
       "broken‐down  time" structure pointed to by _t_m, using the format speci‐
       fied by _f_o_r_m_a_t.

       The broken‐down time structure _t_m is described in ttmm(3type).

       The _f_o_r_m_a_t argument is a character string that consists  of  field  de‐
       scriptors and text characters, reminiscent of ssccaannff(3).  Each field de‐
       scriptor  consists  of a %% character followed by another character that
       specifies the replacement for the field descriptor.  All other  charac‐
       ters  in  the _f_o_r_m_a_t string must have a matching character in the input
       string, except for whitespace, which matches zero  or  more  whitespace
       characters  in  the  input string.  There should be whitespace or other
       alphanumeric characters between any two field descriptors.

       The ssttrrppttiimmee() function processes the input string from left to  right.
       Each of the three possible input elements (whitespace, literal, or for‐
       mat)  are  handled one after the other.  If the input cannot be matched
       to the format string, the function stops.  The remainder of the  format
       and input strings are not processed.

       The supported input field descriptors are listed below.  In case a text
       string (such as the name of a day of the week or a month name) is to be
       matched, the comparison is case insensitive.  In case a number is to be
       matched, leading zeros are permitted but not required.

       %%%%     The %% character.

       %%aa or %%AA
              The name of the day of the week according to the current locale,
              in abbreviated form or the full name.

       %%bb or %%BB or %%hh
              The  month  name according to the current locale, in abbreviated
              form or the full name.

       %%cc     The date and time representation for the current locale.

       %%CC     The century number (0–99).

       %%dd or %%ee
              The day of month (1–31).

       %%DD     Equivalent to %%mm//%%dd//%%yy.  (This is the American style date,  very
              confusing  to non‐Americans, especially since %%dd//%%mm//%%yy is widely
              used in Europe.  The ISO 8601 standard format is %%YY--%%mm--%%dd.)

       %%HH     The hour (0–23).

       %%II     The hour on a 12‐hour clock (1–12).

       %%jj     The day number in the year (1–366).

       %%mm     The month number (1–12).

       %%MM     The minute (0–59).

       %%nn     Arbitrary whitespace.

       %%pp     The locale’s equivalent of AM or PM.  (Note: there may be none.)

       %%rr     The 12‐hour clock time (using the locale’s AM or  PM).   In  the
              POSIX  locale equivalent to %%II::%%MM::%%SS %%pp.  If _t___f_m_t___a_m_p_m is empty
              in the LLCC__TTIIMMEE part of the current locale, then the behavior  is
              undefined.

       %%RR     Equivalent to %%HH::%%MM.

       %%SS     The second (0–60; 60 may occur for leap seconds; earlier also 61
              was allowed).

       %%tt     Arbitrary whitespace.

       %%TT     Equivalent to %%HH::%%MM::%%SS.

       %%UU     The  week  number  with Sunday the first day of the week (0–53).
              The first Sunday of January is the first day of week 1.

       %%ww     The ordinal number of the day of the week (0–6), with  Sunday  =
              0.

       %%WW     The  week  number  with Monday the first day of the week (0–53).
              The first Monday of January is the first day of week 1.

       %%xx     The date, using the locale’s date format.

       %%XX     The time, using the locale’s time format.

       %%yy     The year within century (0–99).  When a century is not otherwise
              specified, values in the range 69–99 refer to years in the twen‐
              tieth century (1969–1999); values in the range  00–68  refer  to
              years in the twenty‐first century (2000–2068).

       %%YY     The year, including century (for example, 1991).

       Some  field  descriptors can be modified by the E or O modifier charac‐
       ters to indicate that an alternative format or specification should  be
       used.  If the alternative format or specification does not exist in the
       current locale, the unmodified field descriptor is used.

       The  E modifier specifies that the input string may contain alternative
       locale‐dependent versions of the date and time representation:

       %%EEcc    The locale’s alternative date and time representation.

       %%EECC    The name of the base year (period) in the  locale’s  alternative
              representation.

       %%EExx    The locale’s alternative date representation.

       %%EEXX    The locale’s alternative time representation.

       %%EEyy    The offset from %%EECC (year only) in the locale’s alternative rep‐
              resentation.

       %%EEYY    The full alternative year representation.

       The O modifier specifies that the numerical input may be in an alterna‐
       tive locale‐dependent format:

       %%OOdd or %%OOee
              The day of the month using the locale’s alternative numeric sym‐
              bols; leading zeros are permitted but not required.

       %%OOHH    The  hour (24‐hour clock) using the locale’s alternative numeric
              symbols.

       %%OOII    The hour (12‐hour clock) using the locale’s alternative  numeric
              symbols.

       %%OOmm    The month using the locale’s alternative numeric symbols.

       %%OOMM    The minutes using the locale’s alternative numeric symbols.

       %%OOSS    The seconds using the locale’s alternative numeric symbols.

       %%OOUU    The  week  number  of  the  year (Sunday as the first day of the
              week) using the locale’s alternative numeric symbols.

       %%OOww    The ordinal number of the day of the week (Sunday=0), using  the
              locale’s alternative numeric symbols.

       %%OOWW    The  week  number  of  the  year (Monday as the first day of the
              week) using the locale’s alternative numeric symbols.

       %%OOyy    The year (offset from %%CC) using the locale’s alternative numeric
              symbols.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       The return value of the function is a pointer to  the  first  character
       not processed in this function call.  In case the input string contains
       more  characters  than  required by the format string, the return value
       points right after the last consumed  input  character.   In  case  the
       whole  input  string  is  consumed, the return value points to the null
       byte at the end of the string.  If ssttrrppttiimmee() fails to match all of the
       format string and therefore an error  occurred,  the  function  returns
       NULL.

