NNOOTTEESS
       In principle, this function does not initialize _t_m but stores only the
       values specified.  This means that _t_m should be initialized before the
       call.  Details differ a bit between different UNIX systems.  The glibc
       implementation does not touch those fields which are not explicitly
       specified, except that it recomputes the _t_m___w_d_a_y and _t_m___y_d_a_y field if
       any of the year, month, or day elements changed.

       The 'y' (year in century) specification is taken to specify a year in
       the range 1950–2049 by glibc 2.0.  It is taken to be a year in
       1969–2068 since glibc 2.1.

   gglliibbcc nnootteess
       For reasons of symmetry, glibc tries to support for ssttrrppttiimmee() the same
       format characters as for ssttrrffttiimmee(3).  (In most cases, the
       corresponding fields are parsed, but no field in _t_m is changed.)  This
       leads to

       %%FF     Equivalent to %%YY--%%mm--%%dd, the ISO 8601 date format.

       %%gg     The year corresponding to the ISO week number, but  without  the
              century (0–99).

       %%GG     The  year  corresponding  to the ISO week number.  (For example,
              1991.)

       %%uu     The day of the week as a decimal number (1–7, where Monday = 1).

       %%VV     The ISO 8601:1988 week number as a decimal  number  (1–53).   If
              the  week  (starting on Monday) containing 1 January has four or
              more days in the  new  year,  then  it  is  considered  week  1.
              Otherwise,  it  is  the  last week of the previous year, and the
              next week is week 1.

       %%zz     An RFC‐822/ISO 8601 standard timezone specification.

       %%ZZ     The timezone name.

       Similarly, because of GNU extensions to ssttrrffttiimmee(3), %%kk is accepted  as
       a synonym for %%HH, and %%ll should be accepted as a synonym for %%II, and %%PP
       is accepted as a synonym for %%pp.  Finally

       %%ss     The number of seconds since the Epoch, 1970‐01‐01 00:00:00 +0000
              (UTC).   Leap seconds are not counted unless leap second support
              is available.

       The glibc implementation does not require whitespace between two  field
       descriptors.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
       The   following   example   demonstrates  the  use  of  ssttrrppttiimmee()  and
       ssttrrffttiimmee(3).

       #define _XOPEN_SOURCE
       #include <stdio.h>
       #include <stdlib.h>
       #include <string.h>
       #include <time.h>

       int
       main(void)
       {
           struct tm tm;
           char buf[255];

           memset(&tm, 0, sizeof(tm));
           strptime("2001-11-12 18:31:01", "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S", &tm);
           strftime(buf, sizeof(buf), "%d %b %Y %H:%M", &tm);
           puts(buf);
           exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);
       }

